    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h2 id="why-fail">Why Most Product Descriptions Don't Convert</h2>
    <p>Before the prompts, understand the underlying problem. Product descriptions fail for predictable reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Features masquerading as benefits.</strong> "12,000 mAh battery" isn't a benefit. "Two full charges for your phone — enough for a weekend trip without hunting for outlets" is. The feature is what it is; the benefit is why the customer cares.</li>
      <li><strong>No emotional hook.</strong> Shoppers make purchase decisions emotionally and justify them rationally. Copy that only hits rational attributes (specs, dimensions, materials) misses the emotional trigger that actually drives conversion.</li>
      <li><strong>Generic voice.</strong> Copy that could describe any product from any brand builds zero trust. The specificity of your language signals that you actually understand what buyers need.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing objection handling.</strong> Every product has an unspoken buyer objection — "Is this durable enough?" "Will it fit?" High-converting descriptions address these before the customer leaves to find answers elsewhere.</li>
      <li><strong>Weak or absent CTAs.</strong> Product pages with passive copy convert lower than copy that actively moves the shopper toward a decision.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>AI, prompted correctly, solves all of these.</p>

    <hr class="section-divider" />

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h2 id="framework">The Conversion-First Prompt Framework</h2>
    <p>Every high-converting product description prompt has four elements. Build every prompt around these — the templates below do this automatically.</p>

    <div class="tips-grid">
      <div class="tip-card">
        <div class="tip-icon">🎯</div>
        <h4>1. Buyer Identity</h4>
        <p>Not "shoppers who want yoga mats" — "women 28–42 who practice hot yoga 3x/week and are tired of mats that slip." The more specific, the more targeted the copy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip-card">
        <div class="tip-icon">🔄</div>
        <h4>2. Core Transformation</h4>
        <p>What does the product change? Before state → after state. "From sore wrists during downward dog → confident, cushioned alignment on any surface."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip-card">
        <div class="tip-icon">🔗</div>
        <h4>3. Feature-to-Benefit Bridge</h4>
        <p>List your features, then explicitly tell the AI to reframe each one as a specific customer benefit. Don't leave this implicit — the prompt must instruct the AI to do the translation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip-card">
        <div class="tip-icon">🛡️</div>
        <h4>4. Objection + Trust Signal</h4>
        <p>Name the #1 buyer objection and tell the AI to address it. Include social proof signals (reviews, certifications, return policy) and instruct the AI to weave them in naturally.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

